multi_hand_landmarks has a list of objects that contain an entire hand's 
worth of information. If say you have 2 hands in frame, there will be 
two hand objects in the multi_hand_landmarks list. In each hand, there are 
21 landmarks (aka nodes) that denote every joint in your hand

2) The coordinate system is actually flipped in OpenCV along (and only along)
the y axis, hence the flipped x and y calculations in both the length and angle
functions 

3) Since the x and y positions of each node is calculated based on its size 
relative to the image (likely using pixels), there is the issue of speed changing
when you move your hand closer or further away from the camera. For that reason,
speed is based off of the size of the index finger relative to one of the joints 
on the hand, thus negating the issue of depth changing speed 

4) Additionally, since length is based off of the relative distance between the 
nodes of my hand compared to size of the frame, that means pointing my finger up 
vs pointing my finger left will actually cause the distance to "change" even thought 
it is still the same, as this "distance" is again, relative. This makes sense as the 
frame is much longer than it is tall, therefore if my finger is pointing straight up, it 
covers a larger portion of the frame. 

Funny enough, this actually interferes with the angle calculation too, just in a more subtle 
way. The values are skewed, meaning it is impossible to calculate the correct value

5) An error I avoided early was keeping in mind the fact that I was using arctan to 
