updated to code to include it. However, I realized I actually didn't need it since atan2(x,y),
which is the function I'm using, already accounts for that

6) Another thing I found out was that you want to avoid adding the with socket... code completely 
nested inside the while statement bc essentially that just constantly opens and closes the socket 
which is really inefficient 

7) This problem is a little more complicated (props to chatgpt for helping here). Essentially is it 
because of the "solution" to the last problem, which is having the web socket send json information
and have the http requests nested within each other, which causes issues. This is because the http 
request is nearly instant, whereas the web socket takes a little bit of time. Instead, the solution
is to add a queue that queues json data through the websocket INDEPENDENT of the information

8) This then led me to wonder if the sent information would actually cause a backup of stored info,
as new info is entering the queue is faster than it is leaving 

9) So apparently that wasn't even the issue, and the frame rate between the web socket and http 
request that was causing issues, the frame rate matches exactly. The real problem was conflicting calls.
Basically, there is a line in the web socket code, the ".recv(1024)" line, where essentially the code 
will just wait there and block the rest of the code from running. The solution was to add threading, or 
essentially running two different processes at once in parallel and independent of each other, that way
the web socket can wait for received info without causing the rest of the code to wait for it
